+"""Verified Point-in-Cone witness generator for arXiv:2307.00406.
 
-The native problem is the paper's Point in Cone problem (Section 2 of
-arXiv:2307.00406).  An integer unimodular matrix B defines the bounded simplex
-
-    P = {x in R^n : Bx >= 0 and 1^T Bx <= 1}.
-
-Its lattice points are exactly 0 and the columns of B^{-1}.  Hence the unique
-nonnegative cone coordinates of q are Bq.  The generator samples those
-coordinates first and transports the standard simplex through B^{-1}; it never
-solves a generated instance.
-
-The displayed B is dense.  Generic exact evaluation takes quadratic work, but
-B is secretly a signed-permuted rank-one perturbation of the identity.  Seeing
-that invariant reduces the arithmetic to one signed dot product and one vector
-update.  This is deliberately a Track-B compression task: the polynomial
-algorithm is disclosed, measured, and expected to succeed.
+The instance is the bounded inequality polytope used in Section 3 of the
+paper.  Its integer points are binary-labelled heights.  The heights encode a
+dense exact linear system in base B; the planted cone multiplicities are
+sampled first and the target is formed from them, so generation never solves
+the manufactured instance.
